 "notes": [
  "Capex is cash purchases of property and equipment and excludes finance leases, so it is lower than the 'capex including finance leases' headline some companies quote (Microsoft in particular; Meta's headline adds about $1B a quarter in lease principal). Amazon is gross, not net of incentives.",
  "An independent read of each company's earnings releases matches the SEC XBRL-derived quarterly capex exactly.",
  "Not yet reported: calendar Q3 2026 capex (late October) and Nvidia Q3 FY2027 (late November).",
  "Anthropic figures labelled 'draft prospectus' come from a leaked, non-public draft reported by Reuters, Fortune and the FT. No public S-1 was found as of Sept 29, 2026, and Reuters reports the IPO is likely after the November midterms.",
  "Run-rates annualize one recent period and are often floors; they are not comparable with quarterly revenue and are never plotted on the same axis.",
  "Kalshi's 'any company before Apr 1, 2027' market traded at 32% on Sept 30, 2026 (live API). A widely repeated 62% figure from early September does not match the current price."
 ],
 "method": "Context, not a test. Capex is total cash purchases of property and equipment by Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon and Meta (mostly AI data centers; also non-AI spending such as Amazon logistics), summed by calendar quarter when all four have reported. The capability line is the best METR-measured frontier time horizon released by each quarter end; readings above 16 hours are beyond METR's reliable range, and quarters after METR's latest measurement can miss newer models. Models take months to train on new capacity, so spending often leads releases. Our actual test for hidden compute is trigger X4."
}
